<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#dharmachakra">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharmachakra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dharmachakra, or Wheel of the Law, is a Buddhist symbol representing the teaching of the Buddha, recalling his first sermon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, known as setting the wheel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> in motion. It is usually shown as a spoked wheel, often with eight spokes for the Eightfold Path. In the Dvaravati culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> large stone wheels were set on pillars at temple sites.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dharmachakra.jpg" alt="Dharmachakra" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: bn (User talk:Armanaziz) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka_Chakra_Y.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> noun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> derives from the root dhṛ, meaning to hold or maintain, and means what is established or firm. Spoked-wheel symbols are among the most ancient in Indian history. A wheel symbol appears frequently on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> artefacts, particularly on several seals, and is present in a sequence of ten signs on the Dholavira Signboard. Some historians associate such wheels with solar symbolism: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> the god Surya is linked to a solar disc, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> of one wheel, and a wheel is the main attribute of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the wheel represents the Buddha's teaching, the Buddha himself and the walking of the path to enlightenment. The Buddha is said to have set the wheel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> in motion in his first sermon, described in the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta. Buddhism took the image from the Indian idea of the ideal king, the chakravartin or wheel-turner, who possessed a perfect wheel. Siddhartha Gautama could have become such a king but became instead a wheel-turning sage.</p><p>Designs have 8, 12, 24 or more spokes. In the Indo-Tibetan tradition the eight-spoked wheel stands for the noble eightfold path, and the hub, rim and spokes for the three trainings of sila, prajña and samadhi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> exegete Buddhaghosa explains the turning of the wheel as wisdom, knowledge and insight. A wheel can also symbolise the cycle of rebirth, the wheel of samsara, which the Buddhist path reverses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The earliest Indian monuments with dharmachakras are the Ashokan Pillars, such as the lion pillar at Sanchi. At early sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> and Sanchi the wheel was often a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a> himself, paired with the triratna, umbrellas, gems and garlands. A pairing of wheel and deer recalls the Deer Park at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> and appears on the clay seals of the vihāra at Nālandā.</p><p>The largest surviving group of free-standing stone dharmachakras comes from the Dvaravati culture of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, where Eng Jin Ooi and Peter Skilling count at least forty, about twelve of them inscribed. A wheel of this kind stood on an octagonal pillar set on a circular brick base. Their makers suggest that wheels on this scale were a devotional practice particular to Dvaravati. A 24-spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> dharmachakra stands at the centre of the flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dharmachakra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its people were early adopters of Theravada Buddhism, using Pali and Old Mon in inscriptions, and produced distinctive Buddha images, stone <b>Wheels of the Law</b> and terracotta stupa decoration.</blockquote><a href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a></li></ul></section>
